--TEST--
A `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::methods` roster carries the inherited methods beside the class's own, and a class declaring none answers with an empty one — `rule:core-classes/reflect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::methods
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::hasMethod
// `rule:core-classes/reflect`: a description is of a class, and what a class answers for
// is its own methods plus every ancestor's — the same set a call site resolves
// against, which is why the roster is read off the descriptor rather than
// rebuilt from a declaration.
class Shape {
    public function area(): int {
        return 0;
    }

    private function seal(): string {
        return "sealed";
    }

    public function scaled(int $by, int $floor): int {
        return $by;
    }
}

class Square extends Shape {
    public function side(): int {
        return 2;
    }
}

var $square = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Square());
echo $square->name(), "\n";
// An inherited row keeps the arity the *declaring* class wrote, which is the
// one a call through the subclass is judged against too.
foreach ($square->methods() as Core\Reflect\MethodInfo $method) {
    echo $method->name(), " ", $method->isPublic() ? "public" : "closed", " ",
         $method->parameterCount(), "\n";
}

// The parent's own description is the shorter list, so the subclass's roster
// grew by exactly the method the subclass declared — a roster that answered
// from the declaration alone would print the same two lines twice.
var $shape = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Shape());
echo Core\Arr::count($shape->methods()), " and ", Core\Arr::count($square->methods()), "\n";

// Asked from the subclass, an inherited name is declared. `hasMethod` is the
// whole roster's question, not the class's own half of it.
echo $square->hasMethod("area") ? "declared" : "absent", "\n";

// The empty end of the range: a class that declares no method at all has a
// roster of none, and every name is absent from it. A walk that numbered its
// rows from a count it did not have would print a row here.
class Marker {
    public int $at = 1;
}
var $marker = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Marker());
echo Core\Arr::count($marker->methods()), "\n";
echo $marker->hasMethod("at") ? "declared" : "absent", "\n";
--EXPECT--
Square
area public 0
scaled public 2
seal closed 0
side public 0
3 and 4
declared
0
absent
